course: dictionary
lesson: 3369
title: Strong words: excruciatingly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Excruciatingly is an adverb. It tells you how strongly something happens or how strong another word is. Its first meaning is extremely painfully: «The cut on my knee was excruciatingly sore.»
explain: Its second meaning is informal. It means extremely, in a way that is hard to bear, as in «excruciatingly slow» or «excruciatingly embarrassing». It usually strengthens another adjective, so look at the word after it to see what is being made stronger.
explain: The Camel in the story is so lazy that the writer uses this word for him: «'Scruciating idle» is short for «excruciatingly idle», terribly lazy. Read the words around it and ask: how much? The answer tells you the meaning.
fact: Excruciatingly is an adverb, so it tells you how strongly something is done or how strong an adjective is.
fact: «Excruciatingly» means extremely painfully.
fact: In informal speech, «excruciatingly» means extremely, in a way that is hard to bear.
fact: Common examples are «excruciatingly painful», «excruciatingly boring» and «excruciatingly funny» (so funny it hurts).
fact: A good check is to swap in «very»: if the sentence loses its strong, almost unbearable feeling, the word was doing a special job.
exercise: «The lesson was excruciatingly dull.» Which word does excruciatingly make stronger? | *dull | excruciatingly | was | lesson
exercise: «The dentist's drill was excruciatingly painful.» What does excruciatingly mean here? | Happily, with a big smile | Very quietly | *Extremely painfully | Slightly sleepy
exercise: «The film was excruciatingly boring, and I kept checking my watch.» What does excruciatingly suggest? | The boredom was small and easy to ignore | *The boredom was almost too much to bear | The film was short and exciting | The watch was broken
test: In «The queue was excruciatingly slow», what is the writer telling us? | The queue was very short | The queue moved very fast | *The wait was almost too much to bear | Nobody was in the queue
test: Ben says excruciatingly just means very. Why is that not quite right? | It means happy | *It adds the idea that something is almost unbearable, not just a bit more | It means very, exactly the same, so there is no difference | It is a short word
exam: Which sentence uses excruciatingly to mean extremely painful? | The puppy was excruciatingly small and cute | We ate excruciatingly quickly | *Her broken arm was excruciatingly painful | The sky was excruciatingly blue
